<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Preserving Our Heritage</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Learning How We Take Care of Our Past</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Summarize detailed explanations in academic lectures. Interactive: Participate in group discussions on literature.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can summarize the main ideas in a lecture about a historical Panamanian figure (e.g., "The speaker talked about Victoriano Lorenzo, a Panamanian hero who fought for independence. He was a symbol of resistance against foreign control."). Can participate in discussions on literature, sharing insights from a story (e.g., "In the story, the Emberá villages are depicted as a vital part of Panama's heritage."). Can respond constructively to classmates' views on cultural heritage by sharing examples and asking questions to deepen the discussion (e.g., "I agree that the Emberá weaving tradition is important. What do you think about how it's passed down to younger generations?").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; text-align: left; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b><br>Students are engaged and prepared for the lesson. Teacher introduces and clarifies new vocabulary and structures.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images depicting various Panamanian heritage sites (e.g., Casco Viejo, Panama Canal, Emberá villages, historical documents, traditional crafts like Ngäbe-Buglé "chaquiras," pre-hispanic artifacts). Students are asked to share what they see and what comes to mind when they think of "Panamanian heritage." (e.g., "I see old buildings," "This looks like a historical site.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces 8-10 core vocabulary words related to the theme: <i>heritage, history, sites, culture, preserve, attract, live, became, landmark, important, traditional, weaving</i>. Each word is written on the board. The teacher models the pronunciation, paying close attention to the long vowel sound /eɪ/ in words like 'trade,' 'became,' 'great,' and 'say' (linking to future content). The teacher also models the alternation of full and reduced vowels in multisyllabic words like 'important' /ɪmˈpɔː.tənt/ and 'heritage' /ˈher.ɪ.tɪdʒ/. The teacher provides simple definitions and example sentences for each word in context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the new vocabulary using Concept Checking Questions.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a building is part of our *heritage*, is it something new or old and valuable from the past?" (Expected answer: Old and valuable.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When we *preserve* something, do we destroy it or keep it safe for the future?" (Expected answer: Keep it safe.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a *landmark* usually a small, hidden place or an important, easily recognized place?" (Expected answer: An important, easily recognized place.)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>New language and content are presented through authentic or semi-authentic materials.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Preserving Our Heritage".</b> The teacher plays an audio excerpt (approx. 2-3 minutes) from an academic lecture or a documentary podcast focusing on a specific aspect of Panamanian historical or cultural heritage, for example, "The Significance of the Panama Canal's Early Years and its Legacy" or "The Importance of Emberá Cultural Villages." The audio explicitly uses target vocabulary (e.g., <i>heritage, sites, history, culture, preserve, attracted, became, landmark, legacy, identity</i>) and grammar structures (relative clauses like "The Panama Canal, which opened in 1914, transformed global trade." and "The museum where the history of the Canal Zone is explained provides a great learning experience." Also, past simple vs. present perfect: "The Panama Canal opened in 1914." vs. "It has attracted many tourists.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Audio recording: Excerpt from a documentary podcast on Panamanian cultural heritage and its global impact. The register is academic/informative.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interprets and summarizes information from spoken texts on familiar and academic topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the audio for the first time and answer gist questions to grasp the main idea.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the main topic of this lecture/podcast?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which historical site or cultural group is primarily discussed?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"According to the speaker, why is this heritage important?"</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Students practice the new language in controlled and semi-controlled activities, focusing on accuracy in listening comprehension.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus on Listening.</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Identifying key vocabulary (<i>heritage, history, sites, culture, restoration, villages, landmark, legacy, identity, efforts, preserve, attract, opened, became, continue, restore, relevant, historical, traditional, global, critical, cultural, significant</i>) and grammatical structures (relative clauses: "which," "where"; past simple vs. present perfect).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Gap-fill Listening):</b> The teacher provides a transcript of the audio with key vocabulary words and specific grammatical structures (e.g., relative pronouns, past simple verbs, present perfect verbs) removed. Students listen to the audio again (second listening) and fill in the blanks. E.g., "Casco Viejo, ______ is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, ______ tourists from around the world." "The Panama Canal ______ in 1914." "It ______ many tourists over the years."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Identification & Categorization):</b> Students listen to the audio a third time. They are given a list of sentences/phrases and must identify if they contain a relative clause, a past simple event, or a present perfect action. They also identify adjectives used to describe the heritage (e.g., <i>relevant, historical, traditional, global, critical, cultural, significant</i>). E.g., Teacher asks: "Which sentences describe an event that started and finished in the past?" or "Which sentences connect two ideas using 'which' or 'where'?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction/Peer Check:</b> Students work in pairs to compare their answers for the gap-fill and sentence identification tasks. They discuss any discrepancies, using phrases like, "I heard 'opened' here, what did you get?" or "I think this is a relative clause because it uses 'which'." The teacher encourages students to pay attention to the pronunciation of target words as they discuss, especially words containing the /eɪ/ sound and multisyllabic words with reduced vowels.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Students use the target language in a realistic communicative task, focusing on fluency and interaction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Preserving Our Heritage".</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group discusses the main points of the lecture they listened to, aiming to summarize its content and share their initial reactions. They must use at least five of the new vocabulary words and at least two relative clauses or past simple/present perfect constructions from the lesson. Students are encouraged to use pragmatic functions like "Why is preserving historic landmarks important?" and sociolinguistic elements like "I learned so much about the Emberá community..."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Describe task format:</b> Small group discussion followed by one student from each group orally presenting a brief (1-minute) summary of their group's discussion and key takeaways from the lecture.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connect directly to Theme: "Learning How We Take Care of Our Past" and Scenario: "Preserving Our Heritage".</b> Students discuss how the information from the lecture helps them understand the efforts involved in preserving heritage, and how different cultural elements (e.g., Emberá villages, historical sites) contribute to Panama's identity. They can predict future preservation actions using "We will continue to preserve..."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus on fluency, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion.</b> The emphasis is on students communicating effectively and accurately summarizing the information they listened to, and expressing their opinions respectfully.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment and student self-reflection on learning outcomes.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group discussions and individual summaries. Peer assessment during group work (students provide feedback on participation and use of target vocabulary to their group members).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group presents a 1-minute oral summary of the lecture's main ideas, demonstrating their listening comprehension and ability to synthesize information. Individually, students participate constructively in group discussions on literature/heritage, sharing insights and responding to classmates' views.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of target vocabulary (e.g., /eɪ/ sound, reduced vowels).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of at least 3-5 target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>heritage, sites, culture, preserve, landmark</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence of the oral summary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active and constructive participation in group discussion (e.g., asking relevant questions, sharing examples, showing interest in cultural diversity).</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Name one new thing you learned about Panamanian heritage from today's lecture."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of summarizing the lecture, and why?"</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b><br>Students and teacher reflect on the learning experience and plan for future lessons.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has listening to the lecture and discussing it with your classmates helped you understand the importance of preserving our heritage?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What strategies did you use today to help you understand a detailed academic lecture in English?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in this listening-focused lesson? The visual warm-up effectively activated prior knowledge. Students engaged well with the audio and tried to use new vocabulary. The gap-fill activity was good for accuracy. What adjustments are needed for the next lesson? Some students struggled with the pace of the academic lecture; providing a glossary beforehand or pre-teaching more complex concepts might be beneficial. Need to ensure consistent practice of /eɪ/ and reduced vowel sounds. Are there any students who need additional scaffolds for summarizing detailed information?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> "Today we practiced listening and understanding our new vocabulary. Next lesson (Lesson 2), we will explore a written text about a similar topic, using these same vocabulary words to deepen our comprehension of 'Preserving Our Heritage' through reading."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>